# Make worktree teardown mechanical: landing-script step plus a launchd backstop. 2026-09-12
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/dev-agents.git wave-plans/dev-agents/2026-09-12-worktree-sweep-hooks.plan --auto --retries 1
#
# Why: olympus-platform documents worktree removal at merge time (parallel-workflow
# skill, make worktree-sweep-apply) and it still piled up twice: 74 worktrees on
# 2026-08-10, 41 worktrees and 7 GB on 2026-09-12. The rule lived in prose and the
# agent that creates a worktree is never the one that merges. Fix it in code.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | devops | Two hooks in this repo (dev-agents) so worktree teardown happens without anyone remembering. READ FIRST scripts/land.sh end to end, scripts/local-sentinel-install.sh and docs/local-pr-sentinel-launchd.plist (the existing launchd pattern), the Makefile targets local-sentinel and local-sentinel-install, and in the sibling checkout at ../olympus-platform the script scripts/worktree-sweep.sh and its Makefile targets worktree-sweep and worktree-sweep-apply, which is the sweep to call; it asks GitHub whether each PR merged, refuses dirty trees, and is safe unattended. HOOK ONE, scripts/land.sh: after the final PR in a run has merged and been verified, run the sweep in the target repo checkout (make worktree-sweep-apply from the repo root the script already knows), print its summary lines into the land output, and never let a sweep failure change the exit code of the landing itself; one line of comment saying why it is there, citing the two pile-ups above. HOOK TWO, a launchd backstop mirroring the sentinel pattern exactly: docs/worktree-sweep-launchd.plist with Label com.arlen.worktree-sweep, runs once a day, working directory the olympus-platform checkout path taken from an environment variable with the same default the sentinel uses, StandardOutPath and StandardErrorPath under the same log directory the sentinel uses, and scripts/worktree-sweep-install.sh plus scripts/worktree-sweep-uninstall.sh copied from the sentinel install and uninstall scripts with names swapped; Makefile targets worktree-sweep-install and worktree-sweep-uninstall with help text. DOCS: one short section in README.md or the existing operations doc that describes the sentinel, stating the two hooks and the manual command, no more than ten lines. VERIFY: bash -n on every touched script, plutil -lint on the plist, make help shows the two new targets, and run the install script then launchctl list piped through grep for the label, then the uninstall script, and paste the real exit codes captured directly. Do not install it permanently; the orchestrator installs after merge. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in code, comments, commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Open a PR. Do not merge. | feat/worktree-sweep-hooks

2 | plan-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of branch feat/worktree-sweep-hooks in dev-agents. No commits, no push, no edits, no labels; tmp writes only. Record the PR head SHA at start and at report. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: will the sweep actually fire, and can it ever do harm. Check: the land.sh hook runs after the last merge and cannot alter the landing exit code; the plist is valid (plutil -lint), its Label, paths and interval are what the task says, and it runs the sweep from the olympus-platform checkout where gh is authenticated; the sweep it calls is the olympus-platform one that tests PR merge state rather than git ancestry and never uses force removal; the install and uninstall scripts are symmetric; no long dash, no vendor name, no trailer. Run the install and uninstall scripts yourself in a way that leaves nothing loaded, and paste the exit codes. Post ONE comment on the PR whose first line reads CRITIC WORKTREE SWEEP HOOKS with SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX and line citations. | feat/worktree-sweep-hooks
